#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147226 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147462 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "631bc93f-4596-526f-9652-f35137b60883" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Common Ratio Using a Formula Given Two Non-Consecutive Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "9d8383ad-c617-5b01-8055-214a8c639f12" :question/math-academy-id "e-2175" :question/problem "Find the common ratio of the geometric sequence whose fourth term is $2$ and whose seventh term is $-686$." :question/worked-solution "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms $a_m$ and $a_n$, we use the formula\n\n$$\nr^{n-m} = \\dfrac{a_n}{a_m}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given $a_7 = -686$ and $a_4 = 2$. Substituting $n=7$ and $m=4$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{7 - 4} &= \\frac{a_{7}}{a_{4}} \\\\\nr^{3} &= \\frac{-686}{2} \\\\\nr^{3} &= -343\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by solving the equation for $r$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{3} &= -343 \\\\\nr^{3} &= (-7)^{3} \\\\\nr &= -7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147229 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147463 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4df9158c-01a9-5596-9528-6033296b2925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Common Ratio Given Some Additional Information About the Geometric Sequence"}] :question/id #uuid "7aa4b1ba-4362-5a4b-88d4-25d9ebe2cef7" :question/math-academy-id "e-2178" :question/problem "The third term of a geometric sequence is $80$ and its fifth term is $20$. Find the common ratio $r$ of this sequence given that $r$ is positive." :question/worked-solution "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms $a_m$ and $a_n$, we use the formula\n\n$$\nr^{n-m} = \\dfrac{a_n}{a_m}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given $a_5 = 20$ and $a_3 = 80$. Substituting $n=5$ and $m=3$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{5 - 3} &= \\frac{a_{5}}{a_{3}} \\\\\nr^{2} &= \\frac{20}{80} \\\\\nr^{2} &= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, solving the equation for $r$ and considering that $r$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{2} &= \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\nr^{2} &= (\\frac{1}{2})^{2} \\\\\nr &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147230 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147461 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81a78a4d-f4ef-5c51-b34e-22d3ee51c7c1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation for the Common Ratio"}] :question/id #uuid "84364858-da8e-5241-85ca-ec84057bc6e0" :question/math-academy-id "e-2179" :question/problem "Find the equation for the common ratio of the geometric sequence whose $4$th term is $6$ and whose $9$th term is $192$." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1r^{n-1}\n$$\n\nApplying this formula with $n=4$ and $n=9$, respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{4} &= a_{1}r^{4 - 1} = a_{1}r^{3}, \\\\\na_{9} &= a_{1}r^{9 - 1} = a_{1}r^{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $a_4 = 6$ and $a_9 = 192$, we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{a_{1}r^{3} = 6; a_{1}r^{8} = 192\n$$\n\nFinally, by dividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{a_{1}r^{8}}{a_{1}r^{3}} &= \\frac{192}{6} \\\\\n\\frac{a_{1}r^{8}}{a_{1}r^{3}} &= \\frac{192}{6} \\\\\n\\frac{r^{8}}{r^{3}} &= 32 \\\\\nr^{5} &= 32\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
